{"text": " If you save whatever's left at the end of the month, you'll probably save nothing. Because there's always something. A dinner out a sale a bill you forgot about and by the thirtieth it's gone without one big mistake. So flip the order, save first on the day you get paid and then spend what's left. Say you want five thousand dollars in a year, that's about four hundred and seventeen dollars a month. The trick is to make it automatic. Set up a transfer for the day after payday into a separate savings account so it never sits in your spending money. Can't do 400 yet start with a hundred. The amount can grow later. Paying yourself first is the habit that matters. Do it today. It takes about five minutes in your banking app and after that it happens every month without you. That's what Demure is for. You archive your dream today so you can see it tomorrow and break it down into small steps you can actually do. If you want to try it out I've put the link in the bio.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.68, "text": " If you save whatever's left at the end of the month, you'll probably save nothing.", "tokens": [50363, 1002, 345, 3613, 4232, 338, 1364, 379, 262, 886, 286, 262, 1227, 11, 345, 1183, 2192, 3613, 2147, 13, 50559], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07141205218198488, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 0.0006900944281369448, "words": [{"word": " If", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.24, "probability": 0.7954540848731995}, {"word": " you", "start": 0.24, "end": 0.38, "probability": 0.9984065890312195}, {"word": " save", "start": 0.38, "end": 0.62, "probability": 0.9902045726776123}, {"word": " whatever's", "start": 0.62, "end": 1.18, "probability": 0.9251846373081207}, {"word": " left", "start": 1.18, "end": 1.38, "probability": 0.9993628859519958}, {"word": " at", "start": 1.38, "end": 1.56, "probability": 0.9954546689987183}, {"word": " the", "start": 1.56, "end": 1.68, "probability": 0.998660683631897}, {"word"